thon environment that you  have in which you do your training that  could be jupiter lab or it could be  sagemaker data iq any of your favorite  um  favorite model training environments  and use that python sdk to have tecton  basically generate a training data set  for you that consists of as many  different features as you'd like to  select from the feature store  and then besides the python sdk there is  also of course a real-time api that  tekto makes available which is a rest  api or grpc interface  that your  model serving application that's running  in production communicates with to get  the feature vector so imagine you have a  fraud detection model running in  production behind some microservices  running on kubernetes that wants to make  a fraud detection prediction by talking  to an ml flow model running on sagemaker  that microservice needs to fetch a  feature vector that allows the model to  predict whether this transaction is  coming in right now is fraudulent or not  for instance you may not care about the  user's  most recent number of transactions over  the last 24 hours or five minutes or  what have you  and those are exactly the features that  you'd get from tecton so you've got the  microservice calling out detecton  getting a feature vector and then you  end up making a prediction and decide  whether you want to reject or grant  the the transaction  and with that we'll now show you a demo  that our lead pm here matt bleifer who's  been working on this project  prepared for you that shows how tekton  and snowflake were used to drive a ads  serving model  all right  hey let's dig in so as kevin just  mentioned we're going to take a look  today at how we can use techton on  snowflake in order to productionize  features for real-time machine learning  models  in this case we're going to take a look  at an ads prediction problem where we  have ads that we may want to show to a  user and we want to predict whether or  not they're going to click on it