fline and they're already being used  for this model but i want to actually  add my own using some additional data  sources from snowflake and so the first  thing i'm going to do is hop over to  snowflake here  and i can take a look at the various  data sources i have on the left so i  know from experience i already have  these ad impressions and users tables  registered with techton  um but something new i've noticed is  there's this ipinfo database which is  coming directly from the snowflake data  marketplace  and it has this locations table here  which has  data about different locations for  various ranges of ip addresses  now i know that my users table has user  ip information and so i think by joining  these two together i can create some  user location features that i can serve  to my model  so  let's go ahead and register this as a  data source inside of text on and take a  look at what it  takes to define a new feature and add it  to a feature set  so what i'm going to do here is hop over  to what we call a tecton  feature repository so this is just a set  of local python configuration files that  we use to define our data sources our  features and our services again those  sets of features that we want to serve  for a model  so  here i have this datasources.pi file and  i can see the  declarations for the existing ad  impressions and users tables but let's  go ahead and extend this and add in this  iplocations data source that we found in  snowflake  and we can see the configuration is  pretty straightforward here we're just  telling tekton the snowflake account the  database  schema table etc  that it needs to be able to query this  data source  so now that this data source is defined  we can actually build some features  using it and so i'll hop over to this  features.pi file here  and we can see that we have uh the two  different feature view definitions that  we saw on the web ui here computing our  user ad impression accounts and our ad  partner click counts and then we'll add  a third feature view down here  and take a look at how we can compute  these user location features  so  the first thing that tucked on needs in  order to register features is just the  query that we're going to run to  actually compute these feature values so  here you can see a query where i just  joined together the  users and ip locations tables or data  sources that i have registered in tecton  and then ultimately i just select my  user id join key my time stamp that i'm  going to use for these later historical  lookups for training data generation  and then lastly the  remaining columns representing the  feature values that i want to be able to  serve so in this case the city country  and time zone for a given user  so now that we have this transformation  defined the next step is  to tell tecton how to actually  orchestrate and materialize these  feature values so this decorator up top  tells tecton to use these user and  locations  data sources as inputs  we're going to group everything here  around this common user entity which  also defines our user id join key  we're going to tell tecton that we want  this data served online so that we can  fetch it at low latency and do real time  inference but we also want it offline  for training data generation  we want this rematerialized every day to  get new fresh feature values  and then lastly we want this data back  filled to this particular point in time  and so that's it that's all we need to  define our new set of features but the  last thing i'm going to do here is  actually  extend the existing feature set for this  ad prediction model and so we can see uh  here  we have a simple list of features that  we are already serving to this model and  what i'm going to do is  define here a  new version of this feature service  which just adds in these user location  features that we just defined  so now that i've got everything defined  locally what i want to do is actually  register this with the text on cluster  and so to do that  i'm going to run tecton apply  in my cli here and what this does is it  tells tecton to look at my  local  definitions and compare them to what's  registered on the tekton cluster  tekton will notice that we have these  new  data source feature view and feature  service definitions and ask me if i want  to apply these changes i'll say yes  and what we'll be able to see uh  immediately inside of the  web ui here if we just  hop over and do a quick refresh  is we have this user location feature  view now registered  again we can click into the pipeline we  can see  the full lineage and  transformations that are producing these  feature values so this makes it  discoverable by anyone else in my  organization  and then similarly we can see this new  version of our feature service which now  includes these city country and time  zone features  and  likewise the full end to end lineage  of this  particular feature set including this  user location pipeline  something else that i can also see is  that if we go back to snowflake  tecton has actually registered  this feature view directly as a view  inside of snowflake so  we can see here that this user location  view has been registered  and we can actually just get a preview  of the  data that's going to be produced so we  can see again the user id timestamp and  location columns that we care about so  all of this is registered directly  inside of snowflake as well making it  really easy to  check out and explore the different uh  feature views that have been created  with techton  now let's also hop over to our tekton  sdk which is what we're going to use to  generate our training data and we can  see how we can also preview and test out  future views there so we'll go back to  jupiter lab here and we will open up a  jupiter notebook here and just import  the tecton sdk  and just like we ran these views  directly inside of snowflake we can also  iterate in this environment t